-- DICT-SEMANTICS §6.1 choice-point 2, THE SEPARATING CASE the spec names in its
-- own text: "`C (Pair Int a)`, `C (Pair a Int)`, `C (Pair Int Int)` all declared.
-- At the goal `C (Pair Int Int)` the first two are INCOMPARABLE, but the third is
-- ⊑ both -- (c) ACCEPTS WITH AN UNAMBIGUOUS WINNER; (a)/(b) reject."
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): §6 C1 states condition (c) -- per-goal unique
-- ⊑-minimum -- and §6.1 says "Recommended: (c) as the semantics ... An
-- implementation MAY ADDITIONALLY WARN at declaration time on (a)-violations as
-- an early diagnostic, BUT ACCEPTANCE IS PER-GOAL." So this program is ACCEPTED
-- and `cname (MkPair 1 1)` selects `impl C (Pair Int Int)` and prints 3.
--
-- OBSERVED -- A PINNED DIVERGENCE (#614 S2 / #311 S3, both OPEN):
--   check/run/build all REJECT with `T-CONFLICTING-IMPL` at the SECOND impl:
--     "Overlapping impls of C: Pair a Int and Pair Int b can match the same type.
--      Make them disjoint, or wrap one type in a newtype"
--   The declaration-time sweep enforces §6.1 condition (a) (global pairwise
--   comparability) and rejects strictly MORE than (c) does -- see below.
--
-- Severity is S2, not S0: the SITE's reject set is a superset of C1's, so
-- nothing unsound is accepted. (NOT because "(a) implies (c)" -- DICT §6.1.2
-- refutes that at α-equal heads.) It earns S2 on the DIAGNOSTIC: "make them
-- disjoint, or wrap one type in a newtype" is the wrong fix, and the spec's
-- remedy (declare the more specific impl) is unmentioned AND INEFFECTIVE.
--
-- ⚠️ Owner-visible: #311 records the 2026-07-16 decision to KEEP (a) for now
-- (it simplifies the #203 min⊑ fix -- under (a) every accepted matching set is
-- totally ordered, so the selector needs no resolution-time ambiguity path).
-- So this row is pinned to a KNOWN, DELIBERATE divergence, not to an unnoticed
-- bug. It goes red if (c) ever lands, which is the signal to re-pin it to
-- ACCEPT/ACCEPT/ACCEPT value 3 and close #614 + #311.
data Pair a b = MkPair a b

interface C a where
  cname : a -> Int

impl C (Pair Int a) where
  cname _ = 1

impl C (Pair a Int) where
  cname _ = 2

impl C (Pair Int Int) where
  cname _ = 3

main = println (cname (MkPair 1 1))
